				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wishun" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1204404); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1204404&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608045256" alt="wishun" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1204404)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wishun" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1204404); return false;" >wishun</a></span> <span class="odate time_1364304559 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Mar 2013 13:29</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1738581">
									

<p>Idiots and zealots are unfortunately common, IRL. Particularly thick on the ground are people who are otherwise competent, even quite intelligent, but have one or two mental blocks that make them behave idiotically in certain situations. Once such a person rises to a position of authority in an organization, they tend to fill up the ranks below them with similarly psychologically impaired individuals. At some critical saturation point, the organization as a whole becomes irrational.</p>
<p>I see DeMontfort as the seed from which this particular instance of SNAFU sprouts. He is generally a quite competent administrator, but he has such a towering &quot;Holy Warrior of God&quot; complex that it sometimes overrides his tactical common sense; and he has stocked his org chart with similarly imbalanced individuals.</p>
<p>Not that my interpretation should carry much weight, it's just my two cents.</p>

							</div>

								
					
